reform of the European Union's fishing  policy tops the agenda at the next  council meeting in Brussels the reforms  aimed at bringing fish stocks back to  sustainable levels and providing  citizens with a safe predictable and  healthy food supply while rejuvenating  the fishing industry in February EU  ministers gave a mandate to the Irish  presidency to negotiate the reforms with  the European Parliament and the  Commission this time they'll debate  whether to adjust that mandate so the  talks can be completed agreement is  closed on most of the big fishing  changes but to finalize the package the  presidency needs new guidelines on some  core issues these include maximum  sustainable yields multi annual fishing  plans the obligation to land all  commercial catches and fishing capacity  management among other elements on  market regulation side the outstanding  issues concern mandatory information for  consumers also during the meeting the  United Kingdom wants to know whether the  Commission will impose trade sanctions  on Iceland and the Faroe Islands over  their quotas for fishing Northeast  Atlantic mackerel meanwhile European  Union farm ministers will be briefed on  the latest developments in negotiations  to reform the Common Agricultural Policy  the cat with the Parliament and  Commission the talks are focused on the  four main parts of the reform rules on  direct payments to farmers of common  organisation of the markets in  agricultural products rural development  support and the way the cap is financed  managed and monitored the presidency  aims to clinch of comprehensive  political Accord on the package with  Parliament at the end of June
 

